---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Why This Exists" description="The story behind Belongary — and the warning nobody got.">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 sm:py-16">
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-heading font-bold text-plum mb-8">Why this exists</h1>

    <div class="space-y-6 text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        I started a job once where my manager was fired the Friday before my first day.
      </p>

      <p>
        She was a Black woman, and honestly, that was part of why I took the role. Nobody could tell me what happened. The team was still stunned.
      </p>

      <p>
        So I did what you do — went in quiet, watched, tried to understand the landscape. What I learned fast was that they didn't trust that she'd made a good hire. They sat me in a row by myself. Nobody said hello for weeks. And one afternoon I overheard two of them in the break room deciding how long I'd last. <em>That girl probably won't even make it past three months.</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        Coldest welcome I've ever had.
      </p>

      <p>
        But that's not the part that stayed with me.
      </p>

      <p>
        Months later, when I finally described those first weeks to the other Black employees, not one of them was surprised. Not one. They'd all known exactly what I was walking into. There had just been no way for them to tell me.
      </p>

      <p>
        I've spent my career in HR and L&D. I've run a Black employee resource group — and been told, quietly, that it was <em>too Black</em>. Too much of that language. Too many of those faces in the photos. I went along with it, because I was excited and I wanted it to survive. It slowly became the Everyone ERG, and by the end it didn't feel like it was for me at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        I've watched people get labeled "not a fit," which usually meant they didn't manage up well, or thought differently, or said the thing nobody wanted said. Ranks close. Nothing you could point to. Eventually they leave, and it goes in the file as voluntary.
      </p>

      <p>
        And I've been complicit. I stayed quiet about things I should have said out loud, because I needed the job until I could find my way out. I've also spoken up and been othered for it. I've been on every side of this.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here's what I keep coming back to: the information always exists. Somebody always knows. It just never reaches the person about to sign the offer.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg font-heading font-semibold text-plum">
        That's why Belongary is here. It's the warning I never got.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 text-center">
        <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-8 py-3 bg-coral text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-coral/90 transition-colors shadow-sm">
          Share Your Story
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
